Quantum Computing’s Progress Toward Practical Applications

Executive summary

Quantum computing is making real engineering progress, but its practical maturity differs sharply across the three areas:

1. Cryptography: practical impact is already here—but mainly as a defensive migration problem. Large quantum computers do not yet break deployed RSA or elliptic-curve cryptography, but standardized post-quantum replacements are available and migration has begun.
2. Drug discovery: scientifically promising, especially for strongly correlated electronic-structure problems, but present hardware supports only small proof-of-concept or heavily hybrid calculations. A commercially useful quantum drug-design engine likely requires fault-tolerant machines plus major algorithmic improvements.
3. Optimization: experiments are reaching dozens to thousands of physical qubits, but there is still no broadly accepted, general practical advantage over the best classical solvers. Progress is real but fragmented and highly sensitive to problem formulation and benchmarking choices.

The key transition is from “more physical qubits” to reliable logical qubits. Google’s Willow experiment demonstrated below-threshold surface-code error correction: a 101-physical-qubit, distance-7 logical memory had a 0.143% ± 0.003% error per correction cycle, with logical errors improving by a factor of 2.14 ± 0.02 when code distance increased by two. Its lifetime exceeded that of its best physical qubit by 2.4 ± 0.3 times (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y). This is an important scaling result, but it was a logical memory demonstration—not a large fault-tolerant computer running useful chemistry, cryptanalysis, or optimization workloads.

IBM’s plan illustrates the remaining scale-up: it targets a 2029 “Starling” system with 200 logical qubits and 100 million gates, followed by 2,000 logical qubits and one billion gates in 2033 or later (https://www.ibm.com/roadmaps/quantum/). These are vendor roadmaps rather than guaranteed delivery dates, but they show that useful systems are increasingly being specified in logical qubits and reliable operations rather than raw physical-qubit counts.

1. What “practical” should mean

A quantum demonstration is not automatically a practical application. A useful system should:

- solve a relevant, properly sized problem;
- beat or complement the best available classical workflow;
- include data loading, compilation, error correction or mitigation, repeated sampling, and classical post-processing in its cost;
- deliver an operationally meaningful improvement in time, accuracy, energy, or solution quality;
- remain competitive as classical algorithms and specialized hardware improve.

Three terms are worth separating:

- Quantum computational advantage: better performance than classical computation on a defined task, which may be an artificial benchmark.
- Scientific utility: produces otherwise inaccessible scientific information, even if an end-to-end commercial advantage is not yet established.
- Commercial or practical advantage: improves a real workflow after all overheads and costs are counted.

Current systems are noisy intermediate-scale quantum, or NISQ, devices. Their circuits must be shallow because errors accumulate. Fault tolerance instead encodes logical qubits across many physical qubits and repeatedly corrects errors, allowing much deeper computations. Most high-confidence algorithms for cryptanalysis and high-accuracy molecular simulation need the latter.

2. Drug discovery

Why quantum computing could help

Drug discovery contains several computational bottlenecks:

- electronic-structure calculations;
- reaction energies and catalytic mechanisms;
- binding-affinity and free-energy estimation;
- molecular docking and conformer search;
- molecular generation and property prediction;
- trial and portfolio optimization.

The strongest fundamental case is electronic structure. Molecules are quantum systems, and their many-electron wavefunctions can become exponentially expensive to represent exactly on classical machines. Quantum processors can represent such states natively. Fault-tolerant algorithms such as quantum phase estimation could estimate molecular energies with rigorously controllable accuracy.

This does not mean a quantum computer will simulate an entire drug and protein atom-by-atom. Industrial drug discovery involves thousands of atoms, solvent, temperature, protein motion, entropy, and repeated sampling. A 2024 Nature Physics perspective notes that practical predictions involve drug-target systems containing several thousand atoms at finite temperature and concludes that quantum computers remain far from routine pharmaceutical tools (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-024-02411-5).

Current progress

A. Small chemistry demonstrations

A hybrid workflow for tautomer prediction was tested on acetone and edaravone. Although edaravone was represented initially using 150 spin-orbitals in a minimal basis, active-space reduction allowed an eight-qubit calculation with 80 two-qubit gates, producing agreement with a coupled-cluster benchmark (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-023-00767-9).

This is valuable algorithm engineering, but active-space reduction means the quantum device handles only a carefully chosen small correlated part of the full molecular problem. The classical computer still performs substantial preprocessing.

B. Realistic workflow prototypes

A 2024 hybrid pipeline examined prodrug activation and covalent-bond interactions. Its active space was compressed to two qubits. For one reported molecule, the classical active-space computation took three seconds while the quantum processor took 63 seconds; the full workflow took minutes to roughly an hour because classical Hartree–Fock and solvent calculations dominated (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-67897-8).

The direct quantum/classical ratio was:

63 seconds / 3 seconds = 21.

Thus, for that subproblem, the QPU was about 21 times slower, not faster. The achievement was integration into a chemically motivated pipeline, not quantum advantage.

C. Graph-based drug-design demonstrations

A programmable photonic Gaussian boson sampler performed clique finding on a 32-node graph and reported approximately twice the success probability of classical sampling, with proof-of-concept molecular-docking and RNA-folding tasks (https://www.nature.com/articles/s43588-023-00526-y). This shows that quantum hardware can be mapped onto drug-relevant graph problems, but a 32-node demonstration does not establish superiority over state-of-the-art industrial docking or structure-prediction systems.

D. Resource estimates for harder chemistry

Cytochrome P450 is a useful test case because its catalytic chemistry can exhibit strong electron correlation, which challenges standard classical approximations. A PNAS study compared advanced classical methods with fault-tolerant quantum phase estimation and identified sufficiently large P450 active-space models as plausible quantum-advantage candidates (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2203533119).

The important qualification is that this was a resource analysis, not an execution on existing hardware. It nevertheless helps identify where quantum computers may add the most value: not ordinary molecules already handled well by density-functional theory, but difficult transition-metal chemistry, bond breaking, excited states, and other multireference problems.

Where useful applications are most likely

The most credible path is a hybrid one:

1. Classical systems generate structures, trajectories, and candidate molecules.
2. A quantum coprocessor solves a small but classically difficult electronic subproblem.
3. Classical statistical mechanics, machine learning, and experimental data complete the prediction.
4. Experiments validate and refine the model.

Early scientific value is therefore more likely in targeted high-accuracy calculations—reaction barriers, metal-containing active sites, photochemistry, and covalent inhibitors—than in replacing complete pharmaceutical pipelines.

Quantum machine learning is more speculative. It is being explored for property prediction and molecular generation, but practical advantage requires accounting for the cost of loading classical molecular data and comparing against rapidly improving classical AI models. A 2025 Chemical Reviews survey emphasizes both potential and unresolved challenges in data encoding, variational circuits, and hybrid methods (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.chemrev.4c00678).

Assessment

Status: pre-advantage experimentation and workflow development.

Near-term value:
- learning how to formulate chemically relevant quantum problems;
- small active-space calculations;
- hybrid workflow and software development;
- benchmark design;
- identifying chemistry that genuinely defeats classical methods.

Major blockers:
- insufficient logical qubits and circuit depth;
- expensive error correction;
- state preparation for complex molecules;
- large numbers of measurements in variational algorithms;
- finite-temperature and solvent sampling;
- continually improving classical chemistry and AI;
- lack of standardized end-to-end industrial benchmarks.

Bottom line: quantum computing has not yet shortened a real drug-development program or displaced production computational chemistry. Its scientific case remains strong for selected difficult electronic-structure problems, but practical impact probably depends on fault-tolerant hardware.

3. Cryptography

Why quantum computing matters

A sufficiently capable fault-tolerant quantum computer running Shor’s algorithm could break public-key systems based on:

- integer factorization, including RSA;
- finite-field discrete logarithms, including traditional Diffie–Hellman;
- elliptic-curve discrete logarithms, including ECDH and ECDSA.

Symmetric cryptography is affected less dramatically. Grover’s algorithm provides a quadratic search speedup in the idealized setting, so increasing symmetric key sizes is generally a more manageable response than replacing RSA or elliptic-curve mathematics.

What has and has not happened

There is no evidence in the gathered sources that current quantum computers can break modern production RSA or elliptic-curve keys. Small demonstrations of factoring do not extrapolate directly because useful cryptanalysis requires a long, highly reliable circuit.

A widely cited 2021 estimate found that factoring RSA-2048 could require about 20 million noisy physical qubits for eight hours, assuming:

- 0.1% physical gate error;
- a one-microsecond surface-code cycle;
- ten-microsecond classical reaction time;
- nearest-neighbor connectivity.

The abstract circuit alone used approximately:

3n + 0.002n log2(n) logical qubits.

For n = 2,048:

3(2,048) + 0.002(2,048)(11)
= 6,144 + 45.056
≈ 6,189 logical qubits.

That does not include the full physical overhead for error correction, routing, and magic-state production, which drove the estimate to millions of physical qubits (https://quantum-journal.org/papers/q-2021-04-15-433/).

A 2025 preprint reduced the estimate to fewer than one million noisy qubits operating for less than a week under broadly similar physical assumptions, using improved arithmetic, logical-qubit storage, and magic-state techniques (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15917v1). Relative to the earlier estimate:

20 million / 1 million > 20.

Thus, the estimated hardware requirement fell by more than a factor of 20, although runtime rose from hours to days. This is a warning that cryptanalytic risk can advance through algorithms and error-correction methods even before hardware qubit counts grow.

Because this newer figure is a preprint and depends strongly on assumed error rates, cycle times, architecture, and control performance, it should be treated as a scenario—not a forecast of a specific attack date.

The practical application happening now: post-quantum migration

NIST finalized three principal post-quantum standards in August 2024:

- FIPS 203: ML-KEM, a module-lattice key-encapsulation mechanism;
- FIPS 204: ML-DSA, a module-lattice digital-signature scheme;
- FIPS 205: SLH-DSA, a stateless hash-based digital-signature scheme.

NIST says these standards can and should be deployed now. It expects vulnerable algorithms to be deprecated and ultimately removed from its standards by 2035, with high-risk systems moving earlier (https://csrc.nist.gov/groups/ST/post-quantum-crypto/). NSA likewise expects the transition of U.S. National Security Systems to quantum-resistant algorithms to be complete by 2035 (https://media.defense.gov/2025/May/30/2003728741/-1/-1/0/CSA_CNSA_2.0_ALGORITHMS.PDF).

This is necessary even before a cryptographically relevant quantum computer exists because:

- migrations across hardware, software, certificates, embedded systems, and protocols can take a decade or more;
- adversaries can steal encrypted information now and decrypt it later;
- code-signing and long-lived device identities may need to remain secure for many years;
- cryptographic implementations can persist in supply chains long after standards change.

Post-quantum cryptography is itself classical software: it runs on ordinary computers but is based on problems believed to resist both classical and quantum attacks. It should not be confused with quantum key distribution. NSA states that post-quantum algorithms are more cost-effective and maintainable than QKD for National Security Systems and does not currently support QKD as the general solution (https://www.nsa.gov/Cybersecurity/Post-Quantum-Cybersecurity-Resources/).

Assessment

Status: offensive quantum cryptanalysis is not practical; defensive application is operational now.

What organizations should do:
- inventory RSA, Diffie–Hellman, ECDH, and ECDSA use;
- identify long-lived confidential data and “harvest now, decrypt later” exposure;
- introduce cryptographic agility;
- test standardized ML-KEM and signature schemes;
- use hybrid classical/PQC handshakes where appropriate during transition;
- evaluate larger keys, signatures, certificates, latency, hardware constraints, and implementation security;
- follow standards rather than proprietary “quantum-proof” claims.

Bottom line: cryptography is the area in which quantum computing has already created the clearest practical obligation. The application is not breaking RSA today; it is replacing vulnerable cryptography before such attacks become possible.

4. Optimization

What quantum optimization covers

Optimization seeks a best feasible choice among many possibilities. Candidate applications include:

- vehicle routing and logistics;
- production scheduling;
- portfolio construction;
- telecommunications;
- energy-grid operation;
- protein and molecular configurations;
- supply-chain design.

Major approaches include:

- quantum annealing, specialized hardware that searches low-energy configurations;
- QAOA and related gate-based variational algorithms;
- fault-tolerant algorithms using amplitude amplification, quantum walks, or linear-algebra subroutines;
- hybrid solvers in which classical optimization decomposes a problem and sends selected subproblems to a QPU.

Many applications are mapped to Ising or quadratic unconstrained binary optimization formulations. That mapping can introduce auxiliary variables, penalty terms, and dense connectivity, potentially erasing a hardware advantage.

Current progress

A. Larger experiments

Gate-based QAOA and annealing experiments now operate on problem instances involving over 100 qubits, while commercial annealers contain more than 5,000 physical qubits. But physical qubit count is not the same as independent problem-variable count: sparse connectivity, embedding overhead, noise, and repeated sampling reduce usable capacity.

A 2024 comparison ran shallow QAOA with one or two rounds on 127-qubit hardware and compared it with D-Wave annealers, simulated annealing, and exact CPLEX solutions. Quantum annealing outperformed the tested QAOA implementation, but the scaling of both methods relative to classical algorithms remained unresolved (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-024-00825-w).

B. Claimed speedups

A 2025 benchmarking paper reported that a D-Wave hybrid solver achieved approximately 0.013% higher accuracy and roughly a 6,561-fold shorter problem-solving time than its selected best classical comparator on more than 50 large, dense instances (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-025-01020-1).

That figure should not be interpreted as a settled 6,561-fold pure quantum advantage. The reported solver was hybrid; comparisons depend on whether preprocessing, cloud latency, embedding, tuning, and all classical resources are counted; and conclusions can change when stronger classical baselines or specialized hardware are introduced.

Other experiments have reported improvements against particular annealers or classical methods on specific higher-order binary problems. Such results are valuable signals, but they do not establish a general advantage across routing, scheduling, finance, or industrial planning.

Why evidence remains inconclusive

Optimization is unusually difficult to benchmark:

- Exact optimization and approximate optimization are different goals.
- Industrial users may value a good answer in ten seconds more than the exact optimum in ten hours.
- Heuristics have instance-dependent performance.
- Quantum and classical solvers may receive unequal tuning effort.
- A contrived distribution can favor one solver.
- Classical baselines improve after a quantum claim is published.
- Input conversion and constraint penalties can dominate.
- A QPU may generate samples quickly but spend much longer in programming, communication, calibration, and post-processing.

A 2024 Nature Reviews Physics survey concludes that quantum advantage is theoretically possible in exact, approximate, and heuristic settings, but emphasizes open questions and systematic benchmarking rather than claiming established practical superiority (https://www.nature.com/articles/s42254-024-00770-9).

The most useful evaluation measures are therefore:

- time to reach a specified objective value;
- probability of reaching that value;
- solution quality at a fixed wall-clock or energy budget;
- scaling with problem size;
- total end-to-end cost;
- robustness across realistic instance distributions;
- comparison with best-in-class commercial solvers and modern heuristics.

Likely development path

Near term:
- hybrid quantum/classical experimentation;
- sampling and subproblem heuristics;
- niche cases aligned with native hardware topology;
- annealing for certain Ising/QUBO workloads;
- benchmark and formulation research.

Longer term:
- fault-tolerant algorithms may provide provable speedups for some search or structured optimization tasks;
- practical value will still depend on constants, data access, output requirements, and competition from classical approximation algorithms.

Assessment

Status: promising but unproven for broad practical advantage.

The field has moved from toy demonstrations to nontrivial hardware-scale experiments, but there is no generally accepted result showing that current quantum systems outperform the best classical methods on an important, naturally occurring optimization problem after all costs are included.

Bottom line: optimization may produce useful niche applications before fully fault-tolerant computing, especially through hybrid systems, but it is also the area most vulnerable to misleading benchmark claims.

5. Comparative outlook

Drug discovery
- Present maturity: small demonstrations and hybrid prototypes.
- Best opportunity: difficult electronic structure within a classical workflow.
- Main requirement: reliable logical qubits and deep chemistry circuits.
- Confidence of eventual value: moderate to high for selected chemistry problems, low for near-term end-to-end transformation.

Cryptography
- Present maturity: no practical quantum attack, but mature post-quantum standards and active migration.
- Best opportunity: defensive cryptographic modernization.
- Main requirement for attack: a very large fault-tolerant computer.
- Confidence of current practical value: high, because migration is necessary regardless of the exact attack date.

Optimization
- Present maturity: hardware-scale demonstrations and contested niche speedups.
- Best opportunity: hybrid heuristics and hardware-aligned QUBO/Ising problems.
- Main requirement: convincing scaling and fair end-to-end comparisons.
- Confidence of broad advantage: low at present; uncertain over the longer term.

6. What progress to watch

The most informative milestones are not headline physical-qubit counts. Watch for:

1. Multiple logical qubits performing universal gates, not just storing information.
2. Logical error rates low enough for millions or billions of operations.
3. End-to-end resource estimates validated against real hardware.
4. Chemistry results beyond what the best classical method can obtain at comparable accuracy.
5. Optimization benchmarks using public instances, strong baselines, equal tuning, and total wall-clock cost.
6. Independent reproduction of claimed advantages.
7. Deployment rates for ML-KEM, ML-DSA, and SLH-DSA.
8. Continued reductions in the estimated resources needed for cryptanalysis.
9. Evidence that roadmaps such as IBM’s 200-logical-qubit, 100-million-gate 2029 target are being met (https://www.ibm.com/roadmaps/quantum/).

Conclusion

Quantum computing is progressing from fragile physical-qubit experiments toward error-corrected logical computation. The Willow below-threshold result is an important proof that increasing code size can suppress logical errors, while industrial roadmaps now target hundreds of logical qubits and tens of millions of operations. Yet a large gap remains between a protected logical memory and a cost-effective machine executing useful algorithms.

For drug discovery, quantum computers are beginning to enter realistic hybrid demonstrations, but present devices solve only small active spaces and do not outperform production classical workflows. The strongest long-term case is accurate simulation of selected electronic structures that are genuinely difficult classically.

For cryptography, the threat is not operational today, but the practical response is. NIST-standardized post-quantum cryptography should be deployed now because migration is slow and stored data may be attacked later. Falling resource estimates reinforce the need for urgency without proving that an RSA-breaking machine is imminent.

For optimization, increasingly large demonstrations and some striking benchmark claims justify continued experimentation, but broad practical advantage has not been established. Claims should be judged by end-to-end performance against the strongest classical alternatives, not by qubit count or QPU execution time alone.

The overall picture is therefore neither “quantum computing is already revolutionizing industry” nor “nothing useful is happening.” Error correction, algorithms, resource estimates, and standards are advancing materially. Cryptographic migration is practical now; specialized scientific and optimization utility may emerge next; widespread quantum advantage still depends on scalable fault-tolerant hardware and rigorous application-level evidence.

# Commercial Kitchen Solutions for High-Volume, Multi-Cuisine Operations in Southeast Asia

## Executive recommendation

Standardize the chain around a **modular, three-layer equipment architecture**, rather than forcing every cooking technique into one supplier’s catalogue:

1. **Core programmable cooking platform:**  
   Select either **RATIONAL iCombi Pro** or **Electrolux Professional SkyLine** as the chain-wide combi-oven standard. RATIONAL is the stronger choice where cooking automation, recipe consistency and specialized combi-oven service are the priorities. Electrolux Professional is the stronger alternative where the chain wants a broader, potentially single-vendor kitchen and warewashing package.

2. **Technique-specific hot line:**  
   Retain dedicated, locally certified **high-output wok burners, tandoors and charbroilers/griddles**. Combi ovens can roast, steam, bake and perform some grilling, but they do not reproduce wok hei, clay-wall tandoor cooking or direct-flame charbroiling. Standardize performance specifications and controls, not necessarily one fabricator in all four countries.

3. **Ventilation and cold-side/service backbone:**  
   Use **Halton Capture Jet hoods with M.A.R.V.E.L. demand-controlled ventilation** over the heavy cooking line, subject to local lifecycle support being contractually confirmed. Use **Hoshizaki** as the preferred common refrigeration, ice, dishwasher and multi-brand service platform because it has documented entities in Singapore, Bangkok, Manila and Jakarta.

The recommended procurement shortlist is therefore:

- **Preferred core thermal system:** RATIONAL
- **Best full-line alternative:** Electrolux Professional
- **Preferred ventilation system:** Halton
- **Preferred four-city cold-side/service partner:** Hoshizaki
- **Wok/tandoor/grill:** approved local or regional specialist built to a common chain specification

Singapore and Manila deserve the highest weighting on energy efficiency because their published electricity-rate proxies are materially high. Jakarta has the lowest stated business tariff, but efficient ventilation remains valuable for comfort, electrical-capacity reduction and resilience. Bangkok falls between these cases and offers particularly strong documented Halton access.

---

## 1. Operating requirements and design implications

A high-volume, multi-cuisine restaurant needs to accommodate three different categories of cooking:

### Programmable bulk and precision cooking

Combi ovens are suited to:

- roasting and baking;
- steaming;
- regeneration and banquet production;
- low-temperature cooking;
- mixed timed loads;
- consistent production across shifts and countries;
- automatic HACCP records and cleaning.

This is the portion of the kitchen most suitable for regional standardization.

### High-intensity, cuisine-specific cooking

Dedicated equipment remains necessary for:

- **Wok cooking:** very high point heat and rapid pan recovery;
- **Tandoor cooking:** radiant heat from a clay or refractory chamber, often combined with live flame or charcoal;
- **Chargrilling:** direct radiant heat, smoke and fat vaporization;
- **Plancha/griddle work:** stable conductive heat and fast recovery.

For reference, one Singapore commercial kwali range offers **36.6 kW per wok**, or **73.2 kW for a two-wok unit**, for 14–18-inch woks. That illustrates why ordinary ranges or combi ovens cannot fully substitute for a proper wok station (https://jindu-sg.com/products/gas-kwali-range-1-burner-gkr-1b).

### Tropical-climate ventilation

All four cities are hot and humid. Every cubic metre of conditioned kitchen air exhausted through a hood must be replaced, often with outdoor air requiring dehumidification and cooling. Ventilation must therefore be treated as an integrated system comprising:

- capture hood;
- grease filtration;
- exhaust duct and fan;
- tempered make-up air;
- kitchen air conditioning;
- variable-speed controls;
- fire suppression;
- accessible cleaning points.

Demand-controlled ventilation is especially attractive because tropical climates produce cooling savings when less outdoor make-up air has to be conditioned. ENERGY STAR identifies commercial-kitchen ventilation as the largest energy user in many food-service facilities and explains that make-up air may require cooling depending on climate (https://www.energystar.gov/sites/default/files/dckv_technology_profile.pdf).

---

## 2. Supplier and solution comparison

### A. RATIONAL iCombi Pro

**Best fit:** Chain-wide core oven for consistency, high throughput and recipe governance.

#### Cooking capability

The iCombi Pro can cover a large portion of multi-cuisine production:

- steaming, convection and combination cooking;
- roasting, baking and regeneration;
- batch proteins and vegetables;
- programmed mixed loads;
- some grilling using specialized accessories.

RATIONAL states that different foods can be prepared simultaneously without flavour transfer and that the system can optimize production by time or energy (https://www.rational-online.com/en_gb/icombi-pro/).

It should supplement—not replace—woks, tandoors and live-fire grills.

#### Energy and productivity

RATIONAL advertises savings of up to:

- **70% energy;**
- **50% time;**
- **more than 30% space;**
- **20% raw ingredients;**
- **95% fat and oil.**

Its example compares 5,000 kWh per month with 1,500 kWh, a 3,500 kWh or 70% reduction (https://www.rational-online.com/en_us/icombi-pro/cost-savings/index.php).

These are manufacturer “up to” claims, not guaranteed site savings. Actual results depend on which conventional appliances are displaced, menu mix, loading practices and operating hours.

#### Cleaning and inspections

The iCareSystem provides:

- automatic cleaning and descaling;
- soil-level detection and cleaning recommendations;
- approximately 12-minute interim cleaning;
- overnight cleaning;
- HACCP data recording;
- claimed reductions of up to **50% in cleaner** and **30% in water** (https://www.rational-online.com/en_gb/icombi-pro/funktionen/icare/index.php).

This is highly suitable for standardized inspection records, but managers must still verify completed cycles and manually clean door seals, drains, exterior contact surfaces and accessories.

#### Service

RATIONAL reports more than **1,200 certified service partners worldwide** and says partners complete **80% of service calls on the same day**. Its Singapore technical contact and regional Asia South organization provide planning, training and an extensive partner network (https://www.rational-online.com/en_sg/customercare/rational-service-partner/index.php; https://www.rational-online.com/en_sg/company/about-us/).

**Risk:** Evidence confirms a regional network but does not provide equivalent city-level office details for Bangkok, Manila and Jakarta. Contracts should name the actual local authorized partner, parts stock, response time and escalation procedure in each city.

---

### B. Electrolux Professional SkyLine

**Best fit:** Full-line procurement where the chain wants ovens, cooking suites, refrigeration and warewashing from a broad manufacturer.

#### Cooking capability

SkyLine is a capable programmable combi-oven platform for the same core applications as RATIONAL. It is particularly attractive when bundled with Electrolux Professional cooking suites and warewashing equipment.

As with RATIONAL, dedicated wok, tandoor and chargrill equipment remains necessary.

#### Energy and cleaning

Electrolux Professional reports:

- up to **33% less water** and **43% less detergent** in new wash cycles;
- up to **25% less cleaning energy**;
- approximately **10% less idle convection energy** from triple glazing;
- up to **20% energy-cost savings** from its Plan-n-Save load optimizer;
- up to **50% lower cleaning-cycle operating costs** in an internal comparison (https://www.electroluxprofessional.com/sg/commercial-kitchen-equipment/commercial-ovens/skyline-combi-oven/).

Selected models carry ENERGY STAR certification. The claims are model- and test-specific; some are comparisons with previous Electrolux models rather than with all competing equipment.

#### Service

Electrolux Professional reports:

- **1,900 authorized service centres;**
- **7,000 technicians;**
- worldwide spare-parts delivery in **24–48 hours** (https://www.electroluxprofessional.com/sg/customer-care/).

A Professional office and customer-care contact are documented in Singapore (https://www.electroluxprofessional.com/sg/contact-us/), with a global dealer locator available (https://www.electroluxprofessional.com/sg/sales-and-service-locator/).

**Risk:** The evidence gathered does not establish equivalent Electrolux Professional—not domestic-appliance—service depth in each of Bangkok, Manila and Jakarta. City-specific coverage and local parts holdings must be verified during tendering.

---

### C. Hoshizaki

**Best fit:** Four-city cold-side, ice, warewashing and maintenance backbone; not the preferred primary supplier for advanced thermal cooking.

Hoshizaki has documented entities or representation in:

- Singapore;
- Bangkok;
- Manila;
- Jakarta.

Its regional network page identifies Hoshizaki Singapore, Hoshizaki Thailand, PT Hoshizaki Indonesia and Hoshizaki Philippines, as well as Philippine dealer Technolux (https://www.hoshizaki-sea.com/global-network/). City addresses and telephone contacts are also published (https://www.hoshizaki-sea.com/contact-us/).

Hoshizaki states that:

- certified technicians cover main hubs;
- authorized dealers provide broader national coverage;
- it holds more than **2,800 local parts**;
- emergency service is often targeted for the same or next day;
- technicians can support other kitchen brands, including gas equipment (https://www.hoshizaki-sea.com/services/sea/).

This is the strongest documented four-city service footprint among the suppliers reviewed. It is therefore well suited for refrigeration, ice machines, dishwashers and possibly consolidated preventive maintenance.

**Limitation:** Hoshizaki should not be selected as the sole cooking-platform standard unless its local proposal demonstrates equivalent capabilities for combi ovens, heavy Asian cooking and tandoors.

---

### D. Halton ventilation

**Best fit:** High-volume, air-conditioned kitchens with variable load, particularly wok and grill lines.

Halton’s M.A.R.V.E.L. system varies exhaust and supply air in response to actual cooking activity. Halton reports:

- up to **44% additional airflow reduction** from controls;
- up to **64% total airflow reduction** when combined with Capture Jet hoods;
- typical ventilation-energy savings of **30–50%**, depending on application (https://www.halton.com/products/marvel-dckv-demand-controlled-ventilation-na-en/).

A 2023 dataset covering 618 customers and 3,719 connected hoods reported averages of:

- **43% lower airflow;**
- fan electricity falling from 17,556 to 3,336 kWh, a reduction of  
  `(17,556 − 3,336) / 17,556 = 81.0%`;
- total energy falling from 88,896 to 44,000 kWh, a reduction of  
  `(88,896 − 44,000) / 88,896 = 50.5%` (https://www.halton.com/article/a-smart-hood-cuts-more-than-half-the-energy-consumption-of-ventilation-in-professional-kitchens/).

These figures are aggregated manufacturer-monitored results, not a guarantee for a specific tropical restaurant. Nevertheless, the engineering mechanism is sound: lower fan speed sharply reduces fan power, while lower airflow proportionally reduces the amount of humid outdoor air that must be cooled.

#### Service footprint

- A Halton sales office is documented in Bangkok (https://www.halton.com/locations/halton-thailand/).
- Philippine supplier Foodmach states that it provides Halton repair, parts and preventive maintenance from Metro Manila (https://www.foodmach.com.ph/halton/halton-professional-kitchen-exhaust-hood-condensate-exhaust-hood/).
- Equivalent direct coverage was not established from the available evidence for Singapore or Jakarta.

Halton should therefore be standardized only with named local installers, commissioning agents and cleaning/service contractors in all four cities.

---

### E. Local and regional wok, tandoor and grill suppliers

A locally fabricated specialist hot line offers:

- correct local gas-train certification;
- faster stainless-steel fabrication and repairs;
- adaptation to site utilities and hood geometry;
- easier replacement of burner components;
- menu-specific configurations.

Examples include:

- Singapore fabricator YEK, which makes customized Chinese cooking ranges for city gas or LPG and supports burner, safety and wok-ring selection (https://www.yek.com.sg/kitchen-solutions/fabrication/);
- Jakarta supplier Limars Teknik, which lists both professional wok ranges and clay-lined tandoors (https://limarsteknik.com/en/products/);
- regional tandoor supplier Tandoorvala, which offers electric, gas and charcoal tandoors from 3–9 kW and exports to Singapore, although equivalent local service in all four cities is not demonstrated (https://tandoorvala.com/).

The main weakness is cross-border service consistency. The remedy is a chain-controlled specification and approved-vendor process.

