## 5. Ventilation design for tropical conditions

### Recommended system

Each site should use:

- Type I grease hoods over woks, grills, fryers and tandoors;
- high-efficiency removable baffle or cyclone filters;
- UV-C or equivalent grease-treatment options where locally approved and serviceable;
- variable-speed exhaust and make-up-air fans;
- separate control zones for wok, grill/tandoor and lighter-duty oven lines;
- make-up air delivered at low velocity so it does not disrupt hood capture;
- slight negative pressure in the kitchen relative to dining areas;
- dehumidified, tempered make-up air;
- accessible duct doors and grease drains;
- automatic fire suppression and gas/electric shutoff interlocks;
- pressure, fan-status and filter/cleaning alarms connected to the building-management system.

Avoid blowing cold supply air directly across a wok flame or hood face. This can defeat capture, spread grease aerosol and cause operators to increase exhaust fan speed unnecessarily.

### Singapore-specific ventilation

Singapore’s NEA environmental-health code requires food-shop kitchen exhaust to discharge above the roof where practical; alternative outdoor discharge locations must be appropriately selected. It also addresses kitchen exhaust design at building-plan and pre-licensing stages and requires grease traps to be accessible for maintenance (https://www.nea.gov.sg/docs/default-source/resource/practices-/copeh-2025.pdf).

Singapore’s ventilation standard also includes guidance on:

- baffle and cyclone grease filters;
- UV-C treatment;
- electrostatic precipitators;
- media filters;
- activated carbon;
- minimum maintenance frequencies (https://www.singaporestandardseshop.sg/Product/GetPdf?fileName=260206091409SS+553-2026+Preview.pdf&pdtid=c417dc9a-0cca-4562-a19e-b076c9a4957e).

**Implication:** Singapore requires the most formal early coordination among kitchen designer, mechanical engineer, qualified person, landlord and regulator. Roof-routing constraints and odor-control equipment can determine site viability.

### Bangkok-specific ventilation

Thailand’s 2018 hygiene regulation requires adequate ventilation in food-selling places and clean, durable, easily maintained floors and surfaces (https://laws.anamai.moph.go.th/th/ministry-rule/download/?did=204266&id=71870&reload=).

Bangkok’s inspection material identifies adequate ventilation through devices such as smoke flues, exhaust fans or air conditioning, and requires these systems to be clean, operational and maintained. It also calls for at least 300 lux in food-preparation and washing areas (https://foodsanitation.bangkok.go.th/assets/uploads/document/document/20210122_32302.pdf).

**Implication:** Design for demonstrable capture and straightforward cleaning rather than relying only on nominal fan capacity. Halton’s Bangkok presence makes DCKV especially supportable here.

### Manila-specific ventilation

The Philippine Sanitation Code requires ventilation that:

- prevents excessive heat;
- prevents condensation and excess moisture;
- removes odors, fumes and impurities;
- uses mechanical ventilation where natural ventilation is ineffective;
- discharges without creating a nuisance;
- includes canopies, ducts or fans where required by the health authority (https://elibrary.judiciary.gov.ph/thebookshelf/showdocs/26/15313).

Food-stall implementing rules specifically require an adequate ventilating hood over cooking areas (https://elibrary.judiciary.gov.ph/thebookshelf/showdocs/10/39219).

**Implication:** Humidity and condensation control should be explicitly included in the mechanical design. Foodmach’s documented Halton parts and preventive-maintenance capability gives Manila a credible local service route.

### Jakarta-specific ventilation

Indonesia’s catering hygiene rules state that insufficient natural ventilation must be supplemented by mechanical ventilation, and that dirty-air or smoke discharge must not disturb the surroundings. Higher food-service categories require smoke-exhaust equipment (https://pelayanan.jakarta.go.id/download/regulasi/permen-kesehatan-nomor-1096-menkes-per-vi-2011-tentang-higiene-sanitasi-jasaboga.pdf).

A 2025 Indonesian commercial-kitchen ventilation standard reportedly adds provisions for hoods, exhaust, make-up air, inspection, testing and maintenance. Because only a secondary summary was available, the project engineer should obtain the official SNI 6572-5:2025 text before design approval.

**Implication:** Specify odor and smoke control carefully for dense mixed-use sites, and tender installation to a contractor capable of testing capture, balance and discharge impact.

