## 9. Procurement decision

### Recommended award structure

**Lot 1 — Core cooking:** RATIONAL iCombi Pro  
Award subject to a successful menu trial in which each country team runs actual products at peak batch size. Require equivalent pan formats, recipe libraries, network connectivity and HACCP export across all sites.

**Lot 2 — Alternative/full-line bid:** Electrolux Professional  
Retain as a competitive alternative, especially if its bundled oven, cooking suite and warewashing total cost is materially lower and it can prove equivalent four-city service.

**Lot 3 — Refrigeration, ice, warewashing and maintenance:** Hoshizaki  
Its direct four-market presence makes it the strongest candidate for regional uptime and consolidated service.

**Lot 4 — Ventilation:** Halton Capture Jet plus M.A.R.V.E.L.  
Award only after local service validation in Singapore and Jakarta and site-specific energy modelling. Bangkok and Manila have the clearest documented support.

**Lot 5 — Specialist hot line:** Approved local fabricators  
Use one regional design specification with country-specific gas trains, electrical certification and fabrication. Dual-source burners, thermocouples, refractory liners and control components.

### Final conclusion

No single supplier reviewed provides the best combination of authentic wok and tandoor performance, intelligent bulk cooking, tropical ventilation and proven service in all four cities. The lowest-risk standard is therefore a **controlled multi-vendor platform**:

- one programmable oven family;
- one ventilation/control philosophy;
- one cold-side/service backbone;
- standardized but locally fabricated wok, tandoor and grill modules.

This approach preserves cuisine authenticity while improving recipe consistency, energy management, cleaning documentation and regional maintainability. The final decision should be based on a five-year total-cost model using actual site tariffs and demand charges, a live menu trial, hood capture testing and binding city-level service agreements rather than equipment purchase price alone.

Clinical monitoring and disposition report

Bottom line

This patient should not be discharged to await the denied Holter or a routine cardiology appointment. The appropriate immediate monitoring strategy is hospital-based continuous ECG telemetry—at minimum in a monitored observation/syncope unit, but preferably admission with urgent cardiology/electrophysiology review—because he has recurrent near-syncope, an abnormal ECG with dropped beats, possible symptomatic AV-conduction disease, and two episodes of transient inability to speak.

The normal troponins, electrolytes, TSH, hemoglobin, creatinine, chest radiographs, blood pressure, and oxygen saturation do not exclude intermittent high-grade AV block or sinus pauses. Insurance denial of outpatient monitoring and a six-week cardiology delay further make discharge without a functioning monitoring pathway unsafe.

1. Interpretation of the current findings

A PR interval varying from 180 to 240 ms with intermittent nonconducted beats could represent:

- Second-degree Mobitz I/Wenckebach AV block if the PR progressively lengthens before a dropped QRS;
- Mobitz II or higher-grade block if conducted PR intervals are constant or multiple consecutive P waves fail to conduct;
- Blocked premature atrial beats or another mimic.

The rhythm strip and a repeat 12-lead ECG should therefore be reviewed directly by cardiology/electrophysiology rather than documenting only “sinus bradycardia.” Mobitz II, 2:1 block whose level cannot be determined, high-grade block, and complete heart block carry substantially different implications from uncomplicated first-degree or nodal Wenckebach block.

Metoprolol is a plausible exacerbating factor. The ACC/AHA/HRS bradycardia guideline lists medications among reversible contributors and states that acquired Mobitz II, high-grade, or third-degree AV block not caused by a reversible or physiological condition warrants permanent pacing regardless of symptoms. For other forms of AV block and sinus-node dysfunction, symptom–rhythm correlation is central; there is no single heart-rate or pause-duration threshold that by itself determines permanent pacing (https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2018.10.044).

The daughter’s description—pallor with inability to talk for approximately 30 seconds—may be global cerebral hypoperfusion from bradyarrhythmia, but focal aphasia/TIA or seizure cannot safely be assumed away. Transient speech difficulty warrants emergency neurovascular assessment even after resolution; the American Stroke Association recommends emergency assessment and ideally completion of comprehensive evaluation within 24 hours (https://www.stroke.org/en/about-stroke/types-of-stroke/tia-transient-ischemic-attack).

2. Monitoring that should be arranged now

Immediate plan

- Place him on continuous ECG telemetry in a monitored bed.
- Obtain and save rhythm strips around every dropped beat and symptom.
- Use continuous blood-pressure and pulse-oximetry surveillance as clinically indicated.
- Ensure immediate access to resuscitation and pacing capability; pacing pads are reasonable if recurrent advanced block, long pauses, or instability appears.
- Request same-day cardiology/electrophysiology consultation.
- Hold further metoprolol while the treating team evaluates a medication contribution, unless a compelling indication requires otherwise. Because it is being used only for hypertension, the balance generally favors withholding it during evaluation and selecting a non–AV-nodal-blocking antihypertensive later if needed.
- Repeat ECG after withholding metoprolol and review whether the conduction abnormality resolves. Resolution would support a drug contribution but would not necessarily make discharge safe after multiple cerebral-hypoperfusion events.
- Obtain orthostatic vital signs and an echocardiogram if structural disease has not been excluded. Normal chest radiography does not exclude ventricular dysfunction, valvular disease, or cardiomyopathy.
- Evaluate the speech-arrest episodes through an urgent stroke/TIA pathway, including neurological consultation and brain/vascular imaging as locally appropriate.

The syncope guideline gives a Class I recommendation for continuous ECG monitoring in hospitalized patients with suspected cardiac syncope (https://www.heartuniversity.org/wp-content/uploads/ACC-AHA-HRS-2017-Guideline-Evaluation-and-Management-of-Patients-With-Syncope.pdf). It also identifies symptomatic conduction-system disease, Mobitz II or third-degree block, symptomatic bradycardia or pauses, and persistent abnormal vital signs as serious conditions potentially warranting hospital evaluation (https://www.ahajournals.org/lookup/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000000499).

Monitoring after inpatient/observation evaluation

If telemetry and specialist assessment establish that he is safe for discharge—for example, no advanced block, no significant pauses, symptoms do not recur, metoprolol has been addressed, and the neurological evaluation is reassuring—the outpatient monitor should be physically applied and activated before he leaves, not merely ordered again.

Preferred device:

- A 14- to 30-day mobile cardiac outpatient telemetry system, with:
  - continuous recording;
  - automatic bradycardia, pause, and AV-block detection;
  - 24-hour attended transmission;
  - real-time urgent clinician notification;
  - patient and family event buttons.

An auto-triggering device is important because he may be unable to activate a recorder while pale or unable to speak. The syncope guideline describes mobile cardiac outpatient telemetry as recording for up to 30 days, automatically transmitting significant arrhythmias to a continuously attended monitoring center, and being suitable for high-risk patients requiring real-time monitoring (https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000000499).

A 24- to 48-hour Holter is not the preferred strategy here. He has had approximately five recognized events in one month—three ED presentations plus two home episodes:

5 events ÷ 30 days = approximately 1 event every 6 days.

A 48-hour Holter covers only:

2 days ÷ 6 days/event ≈ one-third of the average inter-event interval,

and events are unlikely to be evenly distributed. Expert-consensus estimates put diagnostic yield for syncope at only 1–5% for a 24- to 48-hour Holter, compared with 15–25% for one to four weeks of external-loop, patch, or mobile telemetry monitoring (https://www.heartuniversity.org/wp-content/uploads/ISHNE-HRS-2017-Expert-Consensus-Ambulatory-ECG-and-ExternalCcardiac-Monitoring-and-Telemetry.pdf). Thus, the estimated yield range for extended monitoring is roughly 3 to 25 times the Holter range when comparing the endpoints, although those are population estimates rather than this patient’s individual probability.

A standard patch that stores data for later analysis is better than no monitoring but is inferior to attended mobile telemetry in this case because delayed review cannot trigger an immediate response to high-grade block. If no external monitor produces a diagnosis and episodes become less frequent or remain unexplained, an implantable loop recorder should be considered. ESC guidance recommends longer recording as symptoms become less frequent and an implantable loop recorder when episodes occur less than monthly and noninvasive monitoring is unrevealing (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8785099/).

Operational requirements before any discharge should include:

- Written confirmation that the device is activated and transmitting;
- Identification of the clinician/service receiving alerts every day and after hours;
- Predefined alert and escalation instructions;
- An expedited cardiology/electrophysiology appointment, ideally within several days and no later than one to two weeks—not the routine six-week slot;
- Direct assistance from case management, the hospital monitoring service, or cardiology to bypass the previous insurance failure;
- A written medication plan and clear emergency precautions;
- Family supervision and avoidance of driving, ladders, swimming alone, or operating machinery until the cause is clarified.

3. Admission thresholds

Admission to telemetry, with urgent cardiology/electrophysiology involvement, is warranted for any of the following:

Rhythm or ECG findings

- Mobitz II second-degree AV block;
- High-grade AV block, including two or more consecutive nonconducted P waves;
- Third-degree/complete AV block;
- New 2:1 AV block when the level of block cannot be shown to be benign;
- Symptomatic Mobitz I block or another conduction abnormality temporally associated with near-syncope;
- Significant sinus arrest or pauses, especially if symptomatic;
- Alternating bundle-branch block, new bifascicular/trifascicular-pattern disease, or wide-QRS escape rhythms;
- Sustained ventricular tachycardia or another clinically important tachyarrhythmia.

Clinical instability or serious symptoms

- Recurrent syncope/near-syncope while in the ED or observation area;
- Hypotension, altered mental status, signs of shock, ischemic chest discomfort, or acute heart failure accompanying bradycardia;
- Injury from syncope;
- Persistent heart rate below 50/min when associated with symptoms or poor perfusion;
- Need for atropine, dopamine/epinephrine infusion, transcutaneous pacing, or temporary transvenous pacing;
- Inability to ambulate safely or reproduce symptoms with minimal exertion;
- Suspected TIA/stroke that cannot receive a complete urgent evaluation through a reliable pathway.

The AHA bradycardia algorithm uses heart rate typically below 50/min plus cardiopulmonary compromise—hypotension, acute altered mental status, shock, ischemic chest discomfort, or acute heart failure—to identify clinically significant bradycardia requiring urgent treatment and possible pacing (https://www.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Accessible/Algorithm-ACLS-Bradycardia-LngDscrp-250725-Ed.pdf).

Importantly, there is no evidence-based universal pause or heart-rate cutoff that makes an otherwise stable patient a pacemaker candidate. A pause of approximately three seconds or severe sustained bradycardia is commonly used as a monitor alert and should prompt immediate review, but admission and pacing decisions depend on symptoms, mechanism, awake versus sleep occurrence, reversible causes, and the type/site of block. The ACC/AHA/HRS guideline explicitly states that sinus-node dysfunction has no established minimum rate or pause duration for permanent pacing (https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000000627).

4. When expedited outpatient evaluation may be acceptable

Discharge with expedited outpatient management may be reasonable only when all of the following are satisfied:

- The ECG has been confidently classified as benign first-degree AV delay, asymptomatic sinus bradycardia, blocked premature atrial beats, or nodal Mobitz I without evidence of infranodal or high-grade block;
- No symptom occurs during an observed conduction abnormality;
- The patient remains hemodynamically stable, neurologically normal, and ambulates without near-syncope;
- No significant pause, advanced block, ventricular arrhythmia, ischemia, acute heart failure, severe structural disease, bleeding/anemia, pulmonary embolism, or other serious cause is identified;
- A medication contribution has been addressed and a safe follow-up medication plan exists;
- TIA/stroke has been reasonably excluded or an immediate, reliable neurovascular pathway is in place;
- A 14- to 30-day auto-triggering monitor—preferably real-time mobile telemetry—is on the patient and transmitting before departure;
- A clinician is assigned to receive and act on alerts;
- Cardiology/electrophysiology follow-up is expedited to days rather than six weeks;
- The patient has reliable family supervision, transportation, communication, and the ability to return immediately.

A structured ED observation protocol can be an alternative to full inpatient admission for intermediate-risk patients, but outpatient management is intended for selected patients without a serious medical condition. The ACC/AHA/HRS guideline recommends hospital evaluation when a potentially relevant serious condition is identified (https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2017.03.003).

5. Application to this patient

This patient already crosses the practical admission threshold because of:

- Five recognized events in approximately one month;
- Three unsuccessful ED discharge cycles;
- Sinus bradycardia with actual dropped beats rather than isolated uncomplicated bradycardia;
- Possible symptomatic second-degree AV block;
- Two episodes of transient inability to speak, potentially representing either severe cerebral hypoperfusion or TIA;
- Metoprolol exposure that requires monitored withdrawal and reassessment;
- Failure of the outpatient monitoring pathway and absence of timely specialist access.

Therefore, the recommended service arrangement is continuous inpatient or monitored-observation telemetry now, with urgent cardiology/electrophysiology and neurological assessment. If specialists subsequently determine that discharge is safe, a 14- to 30-day auto-triggering mobile cardiac telemetry monitor should be applied and verified before departure. Repeating the prior plan of “outpatient Holter pending insurance approval” with a six-week routine cardiology appointment is not an adequate monitoring strategy.
